  "query_preview": "..Today...Sub-tropical moisture has surged northward \nas a result of Hurricane Marie (~800 miles W of the tip of the \nBaja California peninsula). A band of showers and embedded \nthunderstorms has moved northward through northern Arizona at the \nleading edge of the moisture surge, largely driven by large-scale \nascent ahead of an approaching shortwave. As the shortwave ejects \noff the Pacific, the band begins will stall through the remainder \nof the day. Much of the latest hi-res guidance shows it stalling \nover northwestern Arizona, largely to the west of our CWA, just \nbrushing northwestern Coconino County. Elsewhere, mainly dry \nconditions are expected, outside of a few isolated showers an \nthunderstorms along the higher terrain. Rainfall amounts so far \nthis morning have been fairly minimal, with only a few hundredths \nto a couple tenths in the steadier areas of precipitation. \nTherefore, the threat for any strong thunderstorms and flash \nflooding remains low.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...With moisture still in place, a few isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms will be possible again Monday. Activity \nlooks to however be fairly minimal, as warming aloft will limit the \npotential for vertical motion. Drier surface conditions will also \nlimit how much rain will actually reach the ground.\n\nCoverage for Monday looks to be mainly focused across the eastern \nMogollon Rim, White Mountains, and the Chuska Mountains. A similar \ntrend looks to follow suit into Tuesday, through potentially \nexpanding coverage along the remainder of the Mogollon Rim.\n\nWednesday through Saturday...High pressure over the central US \nbegins to retrograde back over the southwest over the second-half of \nthe week. Much of the EPS, GEFS, and CMCE members have PWATs \nremaining high enough for continued monsoonal activity through at \nleast the end of the work week; albeit a slight downward trend. Thus, \ncontinued daily chances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms \nseems reasonable. Much of the activity should be fairly isolated to \nscattered in nature, and most numerous over the higher terrain. \nTemperatures also look to gradually climb each day, as heights \ngradually rise over Arizona.\n\nThereafter, there remains some uncertainty as a trough may descend \nthrough the Great Basin and suppress some of the moisture to the \nsouth. A strengthening ridge behind the trough over Nevada may also \nfurther suppress upward motion, while also ushering in drier air from \nthe north.",
